              <fr:day>24</fr:day>
            </fr:date>
            <fr:addr>sss-000W</fr:addr>
            <fr:route>/sss-000W.xml</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="Deterministic Regular Expressions">Deterministic Regular Expressions</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p><html:em>Deterministic regular expressions</html:em> are a grammar formalism for defining regular expressions that correspond to deterministic regular languages.

In the spirit of <fr:link href="/bruggemannkleinwood.xml" title="Deterministic regular languages" addr="bruggemannkleinwood" type="local">Bruggemann-Klein and Wood</fr:link> and <fr:link href="/krishnaswami_typed_2019.xml" title="A typed, algebraic approach to parsing" addr="krishnaswami_typed_2019" type="local">Krishaswami and Yallop</fr:link>, inductively define the deterministic regular expressions in terms of their <fr:link href="/sss-0014.xml" title="Nullable Grammar" addr="sss-0014" type="local">nullability</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/sss-000Y.xml" title="First Set" addr="sss-000Y" type="local">first set</fr:link>, and <fr:link href="/sss-0010.xml" title="FollowLast Set" addr="sss-0010" type="local">followlast set</fr:link>:
  <html:ul><html:li><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\epsilon ]]></fr:tex>, the grammar accepting only the empty string.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\texttt {"}c\texttt {"}]]></fr:tex>, the grammar only accepting the length 1 string comprising the character <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c]]></fr:tex> for some character <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c]]></fr:tex> drawn from the alphabet</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[r \otimes  r']]></fr:tex>, the concatenation of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[r]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[r']]></fr:tex> where <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\neg  \mathsf {Null}(r)]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {FLast}(r) \cap  \mathsf {First}(r') = \emptyset ]]></fr:tex></html:li>
    <html:li><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[r \oplus  r']]></fr:tex>, the disjunction of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[r]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[r']]></fr:tex> where <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\neg  (\mathsf {Null}(r) \land  \mathsf {Null}(r'))]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {First}(r) \cap  \mathsf {First}(r') = \emptyset ]]></fr:tex></html:li>
    <html:li><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[{r}^{*}]]></fr:tex>, the Kleene star of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[r]]></fr:tex> where <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\neg  \mathsf {Null}(r)]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {FLast}(r) \cap  \mathsf {First}(r) = \emptyset ]]></fr:tex></html:li></html:ul></html:p>
            <html:p>In each of the above constructors, the constraints on <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {First}(r)]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {FLast}(r)]]></fr:tex> remove sources of ambiguity in a regular expressions. They provide a sound guarantee that the resulting regular expression has at most one parse for any string.</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors>
              <fr:author>
                <fr:link href="/stevenschaefer.xml" title="Steven Schaefer" addr="stevenschaefer" type="local">Steven Schaefer</fr:link>
              </fr:author>
            </fr:authors>
            <fr:date>
              <fr:year>2025</fr:year>
              <fr:month>1</fr:month>
              <fr:day>24</fr:day>
            </fr:date>
            <fr:addr>sss-0011</fr:addr>
            <fr:route>/sss-0011.xml</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="FollowLast Sets in Dependent Lambek Calculus">FollowLast Sets in Dependent Lambek Calculus</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>The <fr:link href="/sss-0010.xml" title="FollowLast Set" addr="sss-0010" type="local">followlast set</fr:link> of a grammar <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> may be captured in Dependent Lambek Calculus via the following proposition:

  <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[ c \not  \in  \mathsf {FLast}(A) := A \And  (A \otimes  \texttt {"}c\texttt {"} \otimes  \top ) \vdash  \bot ]]></fr:tex></html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:date>
              <fr:year>2025</fr:year>
              <fr:month>1</fr:month>
              <fr:day>24</fr:day>
            </fr:date>
            <fr:addr>sss-0017</fr:addr>
            <fr:route>/sss-0017.xml</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="Sequential Unambiguity">Sequential Unambiguity</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>Grammars <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex> are <html:em>sequentially unambiguous</html:em> if the <fr:link href="/sss-0010.xml" title="FollowLast Set" addr="sss-0010" type="local">followlast set</fr:link> of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> is disjoint from the <fr:link href="/sss-000Y.xml" title="First Set" addr="sss-000Y" type="local">first set</fr:link> of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex>.

<fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\mathsf {FLast}(A) \cap  \mathsf {First}(B) = \emptyset ]]></fr:tex></html:p>
            <html:p>We can understand this intuitively by characterizing the behavior of a left-to-right parser of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A \otimes  B]]></fr:tex>. First it searches for a parse of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex>, then upon finding a character that is not in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {FLast}(A)]]></fr:tex> it may begin trying search for <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex>.

That is, there is a unique boundary between the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex>-parse and the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex>-parse.</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
      </fr:mainmatter>
    </fr:tree>
